1
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:04,313
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a question.

2
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:21,663
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure if it's really suited to the format of a group discussion, because it's to some degree personal, but it's about dealing with doubt, especially in the initial stages of the training.

3
00:00:22,744 --> 00:00:23,464
[SPEAKER_01]: I personally

4
00:00:26,416 --> 00:00:29,421
[SPEAKER_01]: I've always been a very avoidant person.

5
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:36,431
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my main way of dealing with problems was to not deal with them, for the most part.

6
00:00:37,372 --> 00:00:40,217
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm to some degree afraid

7
00:00:41,495 --> 00:00:53,102
[SPEAKER_01]: of whether my interest in the dhamma is some kind of elaborate strategy to avoid the stresses of what would be called the normal life.

8
00:00:53,723 --> 00:01:01,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And besides that, another source of doubt are the... we probably heard that question many times... the

9
00:01:02,605 --> 00:01:14,353
[SPEAKER_01]: The supernatural aspects of the dhamma or some inconsistencies in the suttas or, for example, I don't know, are the immaterial attainments the same thing as jhānas?

10
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they really part of the training and so on?

11
00:01:16,915 --> 00:01:17,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Those kinds of things.

12
00:01:18,937 --> 00:01:24,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, in terms of practice being a strategy for avoiding things,

13
00:01:46,248 --> 00:01:50,130
[SPEAKER_03]: The question is, why would you not want to avoid suffering?

14
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:57,893
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, why would you be worried about the dhamma being a strategy for avoidance?

15
00:01:58,654 --> 00:02:03,416
[SPEAKER_03]: It's because you know deep down that avoidance results in suffering.

16
00:02:04,136 --> 00:02:10,679
[SPEAKER_03]: Avoidance results in not dealing with things, which then makes the problem fester, which then makes you suffer even more.

17
00:02:11,179 --> 00:02:14,441
[SPEAKER_03]: So, whatever you do, whether it's avoidance or non-avoidance,

18
00:02:15,077 --> 00:02:17,798
[SPEAKER_03]: It's going to be because you think that it will free you from suffering.

19
00:02:18,638 --> 00:02:26,120
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because that is the fundamental goal of the dhamma, you cannot go wrong by practicing the dhamma.

20
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:31,461
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, of course, like you said, the problem is mainly that pressure of doubt.

21
00:02:31,741 --> 00:02:34,402
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not whether it makes sense or not.

22
00:02:34,542 --> 00:02:41,424
[SPEAKER_03]: The doubt will make you question whether it makes sense or not, even though if you sit down and think about it, it does make sense.

23
00:02:42,412 --> 00:02:49,455
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what you have to recognize is that doubt is a symptom of a non-trained mind.

24
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:54,798
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you will not be able to clear the doubts before you start the training.

25
00:02:55,778 --> 00:03:00,980
[SPEAKER_03]: Because it's precisely the result of the training is the overcoming of doubt.

26
00:03:01,681 --> 00:03:08,964
[SPEAKER_03]: So, if you keep that context of like, okay, well, whatever I do, I'll be doing it because I want to avoid suffering.

27
00:03:09,984 --> 00:03:11,625
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, you will not be able to ignore

28
00:03:12,277 --> 00:03:18,840
[SPEAKER_03]: That all these other ways that you might go about it, that are not the dhamma, are actually making the problem worse.

29
00:03:19,980 --> 00:03:32,446
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you just need to keep bringing to mind that context and reminding yourself that even those things you mentioned, the immaterial attainments and the jhānas, none of that

30
00:03:33,247 --> 00:03:42,172
[SPEAKER_03]: This fundamentally affects the core principle of if I act out of craving, I'm making my suffering worse, which is all that dhamma is.

31
00:03:42,812 --> 00:03:50,096
[SPEAKER_03]: Like all these immaterial attainments, you don't even necessarily have to go through those in order to free yourself from suffering.

32
00:03:50,876 --> 00:03:56,539
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that has to be the underlying context that is universal.

33
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:03,123
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's something that people recognize even outside of the Buddha's teaching to a minimal degree.

34
00:04:03,729 --> 00:04:15,016
[SPEAKER_03]: That if you act out of desires, act out of aversion, you know, break precepts and live an unwholesome lifestyle, you will suffer more.

35
00:04:16,297 --> 00:04:24,463
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's really hammering down that recognition is more than enough for you to practice the fundamental aspects of the training.

36
00:04:26,784 --> 00:04:30,467
[SPEAKER_03]: And the other thing you mentioned was about the

37
00:04:32,295 --> 00:04:33,075
[SPEAKER_03]: What was the second question?

38
00:04:33,695 --> 00:04:55,140
[SPEAKER_01]: The essence of it is that, I guess, the the parts of the dhamma or the content of the suttas that seems uncertain or hard to understand, it also, in some way, casts doubt on what we agree is the essence of the dhamma, the freedom from suffering, restraint and so on.

39
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:56,820
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, that's the thing.

40
00:04:56,860 --> 00:04:58,901
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it shouldn't cast doubt on it, because

41
00:05:01,026 --> 00:05:21,536
[SPEAKER_03]: Inevitably, when you read those things, like in material attainments and all this stuff, you will be reading it through assumptions of... kind of like, I don't know, you haven't been... you haven't ever even ventured near the ocean, and yet you're reading this detailed book about, you know, all the...

42
00:05:21,596 --> 00:05:24,238
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know, the ocean floor and the fish and the...

43
00:05:25,714 --> 00:05:42,046
[SPEAKER_03]: All these things that would be completely abstract for you, then you might wonder like, oh, well, it doesn't really make sense that, you know, you would have this huge, I don't know, heights and stuff that go all the way down to, you know, lower than anybody has ever observed and stuff.

44
00:05:42,066 --> 00:05:42,927
[SPEAKER_03]: That sounds outrageous.

45
00:05:44,128 --> 00:05:51,894
[SPEAKER_03]: And, you know, that's precisely because there is no concrete basis for it to connect with it.

46
00:05:52,314 --> 00:05:54,596
[SPEAKER_03]: But if you put that stuff aside,

47
00:05:55,613 --> 00:06:05,935
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, again, all these suttas are like extreme amounts of volume of information that nobody would have had access to in the Buddha's time.

48
00:06:06,575 --> 00:06:13,517
[SPEAKER_03]: And he wouldn't... the Buddha wouldn't have started talking about these immaterial attainments to somebody who had no basis to even understand what he was talking about.

49
00:06:14,097 --> 00:06:16,978
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's only because now we have access to all these suttas that then

50
00:06:17,958 --> 00:06:24,421
[SPEAKER_03]: All this information kind of starts creating doubts and all these things, but it's a gradual training.

51
00:06:24,922 --> 00:06:29,764
[SPEAKER_03]: The way that it actually unfolds is a gradual training where you focus on what's relevant for you at the time.

52
00:06:31,145 --> 00:06:41,490
[SPEAKER_03]: And because that is the basis where you'll be able to see the direct challenge and how it will help you overcome suffering.

53
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:44,492
[SPEAKER_03]: So, most basically, it would be the precepts.

54
00:06:45,634 --> 00:06:59,374
[SPEAKER_03]: Almost anybody who has a degree of self-transparency will be able to see that on days where they've been breaking precepts, they will have a more wild and

55
00:06:59,964 --> 00:07:02,025
[SPEAKER_03]: An uncontrollable mind, then, on days where they don't.

56
00:07:02,725 --> 00:07:03,525
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's a direct...

57
00:07:03,545 --> 00:07:07,706
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't need to be speculating, like, oh, is this really what the Buddha meant or something?

58
00:07:07,746 --> 00:07:11,927
[SPEAKER_03]: You can very directly see the benefit of doing that for yourself.

59
00:07:12,027 --> 00:07:15,988
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, when you have accomplished that, then you move on to the next stage, and to the next stage.

60
00:07:16,888 --> 00:07:25,250
[SPEAKER_03]: And at a certain point, then, you would come to these immaterial attainments, and then you would see how you practice that.

61
00:07:25,650 --> 00:07:29,711
[SPEAKER_03]: And with the same degree of clarity that you can now see how you would practice the precepts.

62
00:07:30,890 --> 00:07:48,056
[SPEAKER_03]: So, but yeah, again, none of this kind of rational laying out of considerations is going to undo that pressure, that emotional pressure of doubt that you feel.

63
00:07:48,836 --> 00:07:59,540
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's something that you have to keep in mind, because otherwise, it would keep baiting you into trying to resolve it, whereas the very way that you deal with it is by not acting out of it.

64
00:08:03,186 --> 00:08:07,369
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, and you also have to recognize all the other things that you do.

65
00:08:07,449 --> 00:08:27,484
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, if you still act out, like, chase after sense pleasures, act out of ill-will, distract yourself, basically break the eight precepts, that same intensity of pressure or intensity of wildness is feeding into the doubt.

66
00:08:28,805 --> 00:08:29,125
[SPEAKER_03]: So,

67
00:08:30,371 --> 00:08:33,934
[SPEAKER_03]: You will have doubt for as long as you act out of sensuality, basically.

68
00:08:34,374 --> 00:08:52,608
[SPEAKER_03]: If you were to, let's say, not change your lifestyle, but put all the effort into clarifying everything about the suttas, and get a PhD in the entire Pali Canon, the pressure of the doubt would remain completely unchanged, because that's not where it comes from.

69
00:09:02,581 --> 00:09:09,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you mean that doubt never arises again or did you mean that even if it arises, you're not touched by it?

70
00:09:10,343 --> 00:09:18,005
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, I'd say you have to differentiate between not knowing something and then feeling doubt.

71
00:09:18,545 --> 00:09:23,487
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, doubt is a... it's an emotional state that's always bound with craving.

72
00:09:24,047 --> 00:09:30,929
[SPEAKER_03]: So, doubt is when you don't know the answer and you have a resistance to not knowing the answer.

73
00:09:31,742 --> 00:09:35,143
[SPEAKER_03]: You have an emotional pressure that makes you want to find the answer.

74
00:09:36,003 --> 00:09:39,644
[SPEAKER_03]: So, in that sense, yes, doubt would never arise if you tame your mind.

75
00:09:40,044 --> 00:09:41,584
[SPEAKER_03]: But that doesn't mean that you would know everything.

76
00:09:42,644 --> 00:09:51,226
[SPEAKER_03]: There will be countless things that you don't know, but the difference is you will not feel pain, pressure, discomfort on account of not knowing those things.

77
00:09:51,806 --> 00:09:53,686
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's really all that the problem was.

78
00:09:53,866 --> 00:09:56,387
[SPEAKER_03]: It wasn't the lack of knowledge, it was the suffering.

79
00:10:01,496 --> 00:10:06,638
[SPEAKER_03]: Sometimes, you could see that in very practical scenarios.

80
00:10:06,698 --> 00:10:25,906
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you can have spent days trying to figure out the answer to some question, or get some piece of information that you didn't have before, and then, for whatever reason, you get distracted, something else in your life happens, you forget about the question, and then, you try to think about it again, and you don't really care about the answer anymore.

81
00:10:26,946 --> 00:10:31,128
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, that shows you, like, it wasn't actually the lack of the answer that was making you suffer.

82
00:10:32,475 --> 00:10:35,817
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying doubt is lack of knowledge affected by creating...

83
00:10:35,998 --> 00:10:36,218
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

84
00:10:37,619 --> 00:10:49,947
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Yeah.

85
00:10:50,128 --> 00:10:54,951
[SPEAKER_02]: Is there a direction to this clarification?

86
00:10:56,192 --> 00:11:00,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Because trying to resolve doubt and this kind of... you're sort of...

87
00:11:01,385 --> 00:11:02,165
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that is beyond doubt.

88
00:11:02,325 --> 00:11:02,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

89
00:11:17,966 --> 00:11:18,667
[SPEAKER_02]: And I feel like...

90
00:11:18,687 --> 00:11:19,928
[SPEAKER_03]: But it won't necessarily feel like it.

91
00:11:20,188 --> 00:11:20,368
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

92
00:11:20,608 --> 00:11:23,811
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but it is that internal direction which is where you resolve doubts.

93
00:11:23,831 --> 00:11:30,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything external can be, if you tend to it wrongly, become... you will become in doubt about.

94
00:11:30,977 --> 00:11:34,961
[SPEAKER_02]: I just... it just feels like that direction is...

95
00:11:36,711 --> 00:11:39,733
[SPEAKER_02]: A thread for that protrusion I could follow.

96
00:11:40,374 --> 00:11:47,019
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's even in in admitting to, well, coming to terms with, I know that I suffer.

97
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:50,602
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, well, like, I know, I don't, I don't know the way out.

98
00:11:51,743 --> 00:11:52,664
[SPEAKER_02]: I know that I'm lost.

99
00:11:53,304 --> 00:11:58,268
[SPEAKER_02]: And, like, knowing that I'm lost, it's like, I'm less, I'm less lost.

100
00:11:58,288 --> 00:11:58,668
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, yeah.

101
00:11:58,929 --> 00:12:01,971
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, you, and that's a kind of a step.

102
00:12:02,471 --> 00:12:03,953
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I suffer, but at least

103
00:12:04,903 --> 00:12:08,065
[SPEAKER_02]: Having that perspective then allows me to maybe search.

104
00:12:08,705 --> 00:12:15,648
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, yeah, it's it's not even like if you if you were to truly accurately understand, I am suffering.

105
00:12:16,389 --> 00:12:22,452
[SPEAKER_03]: That would already be like as a Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta describes, knowing dukkha as dukkha.

106
00:12:23,559 --> 00:12:26,762
[SPEAKER_03]: You abide independent, not clinging to anything in the world.

107
00:12:27,844 --> 00:12:28,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

108
00:12:29,105 --> 00:12:31,928
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not even that you would need a separate step.

109
00:12:32,989 --> 00:12:44,862
[SPEAKER_03]: If you pursue that direction sufficiently, to see an arisen feeling of suffering on the right level, that would already undo the problem, because the problem is ignorance, so to say.

110
00:12:45,717 --> 00:12:49,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you're feeling the suffering, but you're not aware of the extent of it.

111
00:12:50,138 --> 00:13:00,721
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and yeah, that's the reason why the suttas would say that the precursor to the right view is yonisonmanasikāra.

112
00:13:01,862 --> 00:13:02,962
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not the right view yet.

113
00:13:04,302 --> 00:13:13,905
[SPEAKER_03]: It is something that a practitioner would be able to practice, and if cultivated long enough, which happens through, again, the precepts and sense restraint, they would be able to

114
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:26,245
[SPEAKER_03]: Get it to the strength, let's say, where it's potent enough to properly see an arisen feeling as an arisen feeling.

115
00:13:27,446 --> 00:13:31,887
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess, also, I have a question on that.

116
00:13:32,008 --> 00:13:33,308
[SPEAKER_02]: Is yoniso manasikāra?

117
00:13:39,771 --> 00:13:41,711
[SPEAKER_02]: So, a practitioner can be developing it.

118
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:51,921
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, that brings me back to something I wanted to say.

119
00:13:53,662 --> 00:13:58,584
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yes, that direction of yonis and manasikāra, which is the direction beyond doubt.

120
00:14:00,205 --> 00:14:01,385
[SPEAKER_03]: Anybody has access to that.

121
00:14:02,266 --> 00:14:07,869
[SPEAKER_03]: But, knowing that that is the direction beyond doubt, that's what the right view is.

122
00:14:07,989 --> 00:14:12,351
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's why it's knowing that you know, not just knowing.

123
00:14:12,391 --> 00:14:15,292
[SPEAKER_03]: Anybody can, to some extent, recognize

124
00:14:17,186 --> 00:14:17,907
[SPEAKER_03]: That right level.

125
00:14:18,347 --> 00:14:38,001
[SPEAKER_03]: But being sure and unable to doubt that that is the right level... And hence to go there and sufficiently hang out there long enough in order for that to sink in... And that no matter what happens, like seven rebirths, millions of things that would happen in that time frame, that they're unable to forget that this is the right level.

126
00:14:39,261 --> 00:14:39,882
[SPEAKER_03]: That's the difference.

127
00:14:45,617 --> 00:14:51,799
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on a practical level that there is a pressure of, say, doubt at the time.

128
00:14:52,039 --> 00:15:01,121
[SPEAKER_01]: The direction of engaging with the craving and the doubt is kind of going into that subject of what the doubt is and trying to resolve it and thinking and thinking.

129
00:15:01,882 --> 00:15:03,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And they... so the...

130
00:15:03,798 --> 00:15:15,740
[SPEAKER_01]: The right direction, that right internal direction, I guess, the direction of yoniso, manasikāra, would then be to say, there is a pressure of doubt right now, and then just staying there.

131
00:15:15,860 --> 00:15:17,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that essentially what it is?

132
00:15:17,621 --> 00:15:19,941
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, well, that's what you will try to do.

133
00:15:20,241 --> 00:15:22,922
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if only it were that simple, obviously.

134
00:15:22,962 --> 00:15:28,483
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, you will try that, but you will find yourself still, to some extent, like,

135
00:15:29,901 --> 00:15:33,544
[SPEAKER_03]: The common thing that would happen is you would start to over-clarify.

136
00:15:34,004 --> 00:15:37,146
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so now, what exactly do I do so that I don't engage with the doubt?

137
00:15:38,007 --> 00:15:40,128
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, now that's what the doubt became established in.

138
00:15:41,129 --> 00:15:48,433
[SPEAKER_03]: So, because you can kind of think about it, and it makes sense, and it's like, okay, yeah, that's what I'm going to do now.

139
00:15:49,034 --> 00:15:53,337
[SPEAKER_03]: But when it actually comes up, and then there's that whole momentum associated with it,

140
00:15:54,075 --> 00:16:02,163
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't be able to maintain the same level of clear perspective that you have when the pressure isn't there.

141
00:16:02,964 --> 00:16:04,806
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the tricky part.

142
00:16:06,828 --> 00:16:11,993
[SPEAKER_03]: Almost anything, including your remembering of the practice,

143
00:16:13,444 --> 00:16:14,965
[SPEAKER_03]: We'll end up infected with the doubt.

144
00:16:15,125 --> 00:16:16,807
[SPEAKER_03]: We'll become your way of acting out of the doubt.

145
00:16:17,187 --> 00:16:20,029
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, that's why it won't be perfect from the beginning.

146
00:16:20,790 --> 00:16:34,221
[SPEAKER_03]: But, if you keep doing it, you keep doing it, eventually, these ways in which, like, you... the... your... your recognition of the problem, of where the acting out begins, start to get... starts to get more and more refined.

147
00:16:34,761 --> 00:16:40,826
[SPEAKER_03]: So, then, you wouldn't be able to overlook what you would have overlooked before, which is that even this

148
00:16:41,831 --> 00:16:49,461
[SPEAKER_03]: attempt to clarify what the right endurance is, and what the yonisonmanasikāra is, that too, is falling within the doubt.

149
00:16:49,521 --> 00:16:50,262
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, yeah.

150
00:16:51,084 --> 00:16:57,612
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, you would... your attempt to, let's say, stay with that internal dimension,

151
00:16:58,278 --> 00:17:13,236
[SPEAKER_03]: would more and more accurately end up in actually that, and not, you know, some other distraction that you... That's why it's seeing the signs of the mind is the necessary prerequisite for everything to work, because if you're not seeing the signs of the mind,

152
00:17:14,275 --> 00:17:24,524
[SPEAKER_03]: You won't be aware of that kind of under-the-radar sort of background momentum that's pushing you to do something that you think is wholesome.

153
00:17:24,804 --> 00:17:29,748
[SPEAKER_03]: In this case, clarifying what is the right endurance and not enduring and not acting out of the doubt and so on.

154
00:17:30,949 --> 00:17:34,172
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like a perpetually dynamic thing.

155
00:17:35,092 --> 00:17:36,454
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why it's kind of like...

156
00:17:38,775 --> 00:17:39,336
[SPEAKER_03]: I don't know...

157
00:17:39,925 --> 00:17:44,227
[SPEAKER_03]: Maybe one example would be like, you have to look at the stock market prices.

158
00:17:45,007 --> 00:17:54,672
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, you only... like, you look at the prices right now, and for the rest of your life, you make decisions based on those initial prices that you saw.

159
00:17:55,532 --> 00:18:02,996
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas, no, you will only be able to make the right decisions if constantly you know the current price, which is the current state of the mind.

160
00:18:07,634 --> 00:18:19,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And, essentially, then, when you get to the point where you endure those things without acting out of them in any way... Then, you will not suffer.

161
00:18:19,944 --> 00:18:22,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, basically, that's what the absence of craving is.

162
00:18:22,385 --> 00:18:22,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

163
00:18:22,626 --> 00:18:24,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Being able to not move whatsoever.

164
00:18:24,707 --> 00:18:26,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Not needing to do anything about it.

165
00:18:26,148 --> 00:18:29,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:18:29,911 --> 00:18:33,334
[SPEAKER_01]: But, is it possible that the pressure doesn't arise in the first place?

167
00:18:33,638 --> 00:18:34,399
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

